<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # So Many Different Historical Events to Learn! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> So Many Different Historical Events to Learn!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present personal opinions on a variety of topics. Interactive: Exchange beliefs, opinions, agreements, and disagreements politely.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present and discuss in a polite manner a personal opinion on Panama's history (e.g., "Historians say the canal transformed Panama's economy." "I agree with that."). Can exchange opinions by supporting arguments with facts and responding to peers (e.g., "I believe the Panama Canal was crucial for global trade because it shortened maritime routes. What do you think about its environmental impact?"). Can agree or disagree with peers on historical topics (e.g., "I agree that independence shaped Panama's identity.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting key Panamanian historical events (e.g., the declaration of independence, the construction of the Panama Canal, the transfer of the canal). Students are asked to identify the events and share one word that comes to mind for each image. The teacher then asks guiding questions to elicit brief opinions: "Which of these events do you think had the greatest significance for Panama's identity?" or "When did Panama declare independence?" This activates prior knowledge and introduces the theme of discussing historical events. (Vocabulary: <i>history, Panama, independence, canal, significance, identity, events</i>)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express personal opinions and agree/disagree politely. For example, "Historians say the canal transformed Panama's economy. I agree with that because it created many jobs and boosted global trade." The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in 'say' vs. /z/ in 'was') and the stress for words like 'research' (noun vs. verb). The use of Past simple and Reported speech is demonstrated naturally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I believe the separation was crucial for Panama,' am I stating a fact or an opinion?" (Expected answer: Opinion)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I use 'Historians say...', am I giving my own idea or someone else's?" (Expected answer: Someone else's/A reported idea)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short, contrasting historical reports or summaries (approx. 150 words each) from different online sources about the impact of the Panama Canal on the country's economy and culture. One report might emphasize the positive economic transformation and global significance, while the other might focus on the complex legacy and socio-political challenges faced during its construction period. The material is presented as a printed handout or projected text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand and interpret different perspectives presented in short historical reports to prepare for discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read Report A and Report B silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Oral):</b> "What is the main idea or primary focus of Report A?" "What is the main idea or primary focus of Report B?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pair work):</b> Students work in pairs to identify and match statements from a provided list to either Report A or Report B (e.g., "This report states the canal was completed in 1914," "This report claims the construction led to significant economic growth," "This report discusses the cultural diversity brought by the project"). This helps them pinpoint factual statements and reported opinions, using vocabulary such as <i>economy, transformation, significance, construction, diversity, culture, legacy, period</i>.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Past simple for historical events, Reported speech (e.g., "Historians say...", "The report mentioned that..."), Passive voice for describing events (e.g., "The canal was completed...", "Panama's economy was transformed...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, report, construction, economy, diversity, events, culture, period, identity, routes, engineering, marvel, revolution, transformation, legacy, heritage</i> (nouns), <i>declared, learned, shaped, reported, completed, began, transferred, transformed, compare, describe, reflect, summarize, analyze, understand, explain, explore, gain</i> (verbs), <i>historical, relevant, colonial, diverse, significant, rich, complex, independent, cultural, revolutionary, accomplished</i> (adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice of contrastive sounds (/s/ vs. /z/, /ð/ vs. /θ/), stress for two-syllable nouns/verbs (e.g., "REsearch" vs. "reSEARCH"), and -ed endings (/d/, /t/, /ɪd/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about important historical events, sharing opinions, asking for clarification.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation - Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentences with historical facts and students transform them orally using reported speech or passive voice.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Panama declared independence in 1903." Student: "It is reported that Panama <i>declared independence</i> in 1903."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Engineers finished the canal in 1914." Student: "The canal <i>was completed</i> in 1914." (Practice -ed endings and passive voice)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Some people think the canal boosted global trade." Student: "Some people say the canal <i>boosted global trade</i>." (Practice reported speech, /s/ in say)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Opinion Statement Cards - Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives cards with a historical statement (e.g., "The separation from Colombia was a defining moment for Panama's identity.") and sentence starters for expressing opinions, agreement, or disagreement (e.g., "I believe...", "In my opinion...", "I agree with that because...", "I disagree because..."). They orally formulate full sentences, taking turns to present an opinion and respond to their partner's. (e.g., Student A: "I believe the construction of the Panama Canal was an engineering marvel." Student B: "I agree with that because its engineering was truly accomplished for that period.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Interactive Practice (Historical Interview - Peer Check):</b> Students interview each other in pairs about the historical reports from Stage 2. They use prompts like: "According to Report A, what impact did the canal have on Panama's economy?" "Do you think the canal really transformed Panama's economy immediately? Why?" "Why do you believe independence shaped Panama's national identity?" Students listen for correct grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation, providing peer feedback. This emphasizes asking for clarification and sharing opinions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group "Historical Debate Cafe"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of "debate prompts" related to Panamanian historical events and their significance (e.g., "The U.S. involvement in the canal's construction caused political tensions for years," "The separation from Colombia in 1903 was a turning point for Panama's development," "Panama's rich cultural diversity was greatly influenced by the canal project.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. For each prompt, students must discuss their personal opinions, using the target language for agreeing and disagreeing politely. They are encouraged to support their arguments with facts (as learned in previous lessons or from the provided reports/prompts) and to ask their peers for clarification or further opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Students must consciously use Past simple, Reported speech (e.g., "Some historians believe...", "It is often said that..."), and Passive voice (e.g., "The economy was greatly influenced...", "Independence was declared...") during their discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher circulates, observing discussions, providing support, and noting instances of effective communication and areas for improvement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example interaction:</b> Student A: "I believe the Panama Canal was crucial for global trade because it shortened maritime routes. What do you think about its environmental impact?" Student B: "I agree that its economic significance is undeniable. However, I read that its construction had a complex environmental legacy." Student C: "Historians say the canal also fostered a diverse culture, which is important for our national identity."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment (brief).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' active participation and quality of interaction during the "Historical Debate Cafe" (Stage 4) will be assessed. Peers will also provide brief feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially -ed endings, contrastive sounds, word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary related to history and events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Past simple, Reported speech, and Passive voice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in presenting personal opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to politely agree or disagree and support arguments with facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and interactive engagement with peers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new historical fact or opinion you learned from your classmates today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1-5 (1=not confident, 5=very confident), how confident were you in expressing your opinions about historical events in English today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about expressing your opinions on historical events in English, and what is one specific thing you can do to improve for next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which phrases for agreeing or disagreeing with others did you find most useful today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Historical Debate Cafe" activity effectively encouraged students to use the target language for opinion exchange and argumentation. Most students actively participated and utilized the Past simple and Reported speech, though some struggled with consistently applying Passive voice. Pronunciation of -ed endings improved, but more focus on stress for two-syllable nouns/verbs is needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The semi-controlled practice (Opinion Statement Cards) was a strong bridge to the production stage, providing necessary scaffolding. I need to ensure clearer connections between historical facts and the opinions derived from them.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The rich discussions today helped us practice articulating and exchanging our opinions on historical events. In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), we will transition from spoken opinions to written ones, learning how to construct well-supported argumentative paragraphs about these historical topics, building on the vocabulary and grammatical structures (Past simple, Reported speech, Passive voice) we used today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>